  "strategy": "approach\nWe combine multiple approaches to find a better sequence. First, we generate initial diverse sequences including uniform, sine, Gaussian, bimodal, and random sequences with different lengths. Then, we use an adaptive optimization method that combines mirror descent, projected gradient descent, and simulated annealing.\n\nFor the optimization, we use a five-phase procedure for each initial sequence:\n1. Initial mirror descent to optimize the sequence within 30% of the budget.\n2. Projected gradient descent to refine the sequence further.\n3. Second mirror descent run with a fresh simplex projection.\n4. Random simulated annealing to explore potentially better solutions.\n5. Final mirror descent with prioritized simplex projection.\n\nThe annealing phase is particularly important as it automatically checks if a new sequence is feasible. The optimization method is tailored to keep the sum at a suitable scale and avoid negative values.\n\nFinally, we allow variations in length (from 256 to larger) and content (sprinkling noise or random perturbations) influenced by the objective score, in order to steer the search toward promising areas.\n\nmathematical-specification\nExact objective:\nscore = 2 * n * (convolve(a, a, mode='full')).max() / (sum(a) ** 2)\n\nConstraints:\n- Each element must be non-negative (we enforce via project_simplex and other projection functions)\n- The sequence must have a sum at least 0.01 (enforced by project_simplex when the sum is too low)\n\nImplementation:\nWe use toposort method with adaptive simplex projections and coordinate-wise optimization. The annealing phase is guided by gradient-based uphill moves when slightly beneficial.\n\nSurrogate objective:\nobj = 2 * n * (convolve(a, a, mode='full')).max() / (sum(a) ** 2)\nhandled by multiple optimization methods listed below.\n\nimplementation-procedure\n0. Set initial budget fractions (30% for init, 60% for main optimization).\n1. Generate candidate sequences with lengths: 256, 512, 1024, and 4096 if time allows.\n2. Apply optimization phases sequentially until budget is exhausted.\n3. Determine best sequence by directly evaluating with `evaluate_sequence`, not surrogates alone.\n\nAlgorithm Components:\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.optimize import minimize\n\ndef newton_step(x, iter_num):\n    # Custom Newton-like method\n    # ...\n\ndef project_simplex(v):\n    # Standard simplex projection (with positivity and floor sum check)\n    # ...\n\ndef fast_convolve(a, mode='full'):\n    # Improved FFT convolution with robustness\n    # ...\n\ndef anneal_explore(a, lr_max=0.05, max_iters=1000):\n    # Simulated annealing with custom cooling and exploration\n    # ...\n\ndef optimize_mirror_step(func, time_limit, eta_init=0.5, centroid=False, dimension=None):\n    # Mirror descent step as before but with dimension-aware optimization\n    # ...\n\ndef optimize_pgd(func, time_limit, lr_init=0.05):\n    # Projected gradient descent with coordinate-wise updates\n    # ...\n\ndef optimize_simulated_annealing(a, time_limit):\n    # New annealing step that balances between exploration and improvement\n    # ...\n\ndef get_parent():\n    # Load parent sequence if available, normalize to sum=1, clip to [0,1000]\n    # ...\n\ndef is_sequence_valid(seq_tuple):\n    # Check if a sequence is valid (non-empty, all finite, non-negative)\n    # ...\n\ndef evaluate_sequence(seq):\n    # Full evaluation with safety checks\n    # ...\n\ndef record_best(seq, best_list):\n    # Record and update best sequence based on objective score\n    # ...\n\ndef propose_candidate(seed=42, budget_s=2400, parent=None):\n    # Main function orchestrating the search\n    # ...\n\n    best_sequence = None\n    best_score = float('inf')\n    deadline = time_limit_total  # Dynamically calculate from available time\n\n    # Generate initial candidates\n    candidates = []\n    n_init = 256  # Standard starting point\n    dataset_sizes = [512, 1024, 4096]  #